---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import logo from "../assets/brand/logo-lockup.png";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<Base
  title="The name"
  description="Sophandros joins sophos, wise, and andros, man. The name is for a modern revival of the classical education: the progression of ideals, and why we do what we do today."
>
  <header class="page-head wrap-narrow">
    <p class="kicker">σοφός · ἀνήρ</p>
    <h1>The wise man</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Sophandros is a coined name for a modern revival of the classical education.
      It joins two Greek words, and it is not itself a word of ancient Greek.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <figure class="figure figure-mark about-lockup">
        <Image
          src={logo}
          alt="Sophandros: a gold line profile in a thin gold circle, with the name in classical capitals."
          width={923}
          height={524}
          class="about-logo"
        />
        <figcaption>The mark: a profile in a gold circle, and the name.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="etymology">
        <article>
          <p class="greek" lang="grc">σοφός</p>
          <p class="etym-gloss"><em>sophos</em>, wise.</p>
          <p>
            The adjective is the skilled or the wise person. It is the first half
            of the name.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="greek" lang="grc">ἀνήρ</p>
          <p class="etym-gloss"><em>anēr</em>, man. The genitive is <em>andros</em>.</p>
          <p>
            In a Greek compound the word takes that stem, so the ending is
            <em>-andros</em>.
          </p>
        </article>
        <p class="etym-join">
          <span class="etym-sign" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
          Sophandros is the wise man: the figure this reading is forming.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The mark draws that person as a profile in gold, with the name set
          in classical capitals.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow prose">
      <h2>What the name is for</h2>
      <p>
        The revival is a way of understanding the progression of human ideals, and
        of the inventions and paradigm shifts that followed them. Read in order,
        the books show why a form failed and why another prospered, and why we do
        what we do today. A professional already has a long education. What it
        often leaves out is that progression.
      </p>
      <p>
        The order is the old one: Homer before the historians, the historians
        before the tragedians, Plato before Aristotle, Greece before Rome, Rome
        before the Renaissance. The American Founders used this kind of reading
        when they argued about a republic, taking some things from the old
        constitutions and refusing others. That use is written out in the
        <a href="/founders">Founders Edition</a>. The full course is the same habit,
        carried through to a working life now.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sophandros sets the sequence at a working pace. It is modelled on the
        humanist education of the European nobility and on the Grand Tour, rebuilt
        as self-paced study. Online work runs through the year. Travel, when it
        happens, is a capstone, after the principles are already in hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        The weekly writing is Ascham’s double translation, from <em>The Scholemaster</em>
        (1570). The order of subjects, words studied beside things, is Milton’s, from
        <em>Of Education</em> (1644). Both are described on the <a href="/">home page</a>,
        and carried out in the <a href="/curriculum">Greece syllabus</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow prose">
      <h2>Write to us</h2>
      <p>
        {site.name} · {site.domain}
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a preview of the public site. The Greece syllabi are drafts. Rome
        and the Renaissance are not yet written. Enrollment is not open.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button" href="/waitlist">Join the waitlist</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
